/* DEC15 · Week 4 — protected course texts (copied exactly from the Teacher's Book; do not edit).
   Headings inside a text start with "## ". Added to any texts already loaded (a day can load Week 3 + Week 4). */
window.DEC15_SOURCES = (window.DEC15_SOURCES || []).filter(s => !["gunders", "castro", "huang", "negotiation-ending2"].includes(s.id)).concat([
  {
    "id": "gunders",
    "short": "Listening · Gunders",
    "title": "How to turn the tables on food waste",
    "cite": "Gunders (2024)",
    "kind": "Core listening transcript",
    "reference": "Adapted from: Gunders, D. (2024, June). How to turn the tables on food waste [Video]. TED Conferences. https://www.ted.com/talks/dana_gunders_how_to_turn_the_tables_on_food_waste",
    "citeAs": "Cite as Gunders (2024) or (Gunders, 2024).",
    "note": "Dana Gunders works at ReFED, an organisation dedicated to reducing food waste. Listen first; then read to check.",
    "paragraphs": [
      "A. For the past 15 years, I have been obsessed with the amount of food we waste. This makes me like the last person anyone wants to have dinner with. Inevitably, we’re sitting there at the end of the meal, they’re pushing food around their plate they don’t want to eat, and they’re looking at me with some awkward excuse. And I say, look, we can’t eat our way out of this. This is a systems problem, and it’s just way too big. How big? Well, picture a farm. It’s the size of the entire United States. It uses three times as much water as the whole country, and it grows food all year long, and when harvested, produces enough to fill 100 tractor trailers every minute all year long. Those trucks then drive, fly, and float all over the world. Except instead of going somewhere to be eaten, they go straight to a landfill where the food rots and produces methane, a powerful greenhouse gas.",
      "B. Seems crazy, right? But that’s effectively what we’re doing. From science experiments in the back of our refrigerators to truckloads of products that are too close to some arbitrary expiration date, globally, one billion meals go uneaten every single day. That’s more than a meal per person for everyone on this planet who faces hunger. Not to mention it’s worth one trillion dollars. And this whole ridiculous exercise has five times the greenhouse gas footprint of the entire aviation industry.",
      "C. Now, I know it’s not obvious why food waste would have such a big climate impact, so let me explain. First, landfills. Landfills are the third-largest source of methane in the US, and almost 60 percent of that methane is coming from food rotting. And as big as that is, it’s dwarfed by the huge amount of energy and resources it takes to grow, harvest, transport, cool, cook food and get it to our tables.",
      "D. And there’s an even larger reason. And that’s land use. We are looking ahead at a future in 2050 where it’s projected we’ll need about 50 percent more food than we had in 2010. And the question is, where is that food going to come from? Are we going to cut down more rainforests to grow it, or are we going to use the food that we already have? Researchers estimate about 20 percent of that gap could be met by simply wasting less.",
      "E. When I first came across these numbers, I thought to myself, gosh, this is the dumbest problem. And I wanted to know why. And it turns out, there are all sorts of reasons that are very different for a strawberry farm in California than a market in Africa. But to give you a sense, here are a few. One is we don’t measure it, so it’s invisible. And as we all know, you don’t manage what you don’t measure. Also, in some places, food is relatively cheap and the fees to throw it away are even cheaper. In other places, there’s simply not the infrastructure to keep food cold. At times, we’re worried about running out because God forbid I don’t have enough stuffed mushrooms for everyone. And then at other times, we’re worried about it making us sick. When in doubt, throw it out, right? And then there’s my seven-year-old son who begs me for a peanut butter and jelly sandwich in the morning and comes home in the afternoon with his lunchbox completely untouched. And when I ask him what he ate for lunch, he says, \"Oh, I just talked.\" Now, I don’t know what you do about that one.",
      "F. But overall, fixing food waste is not rocket science. It’s really just about managing our food better, and it’s solvable. There’s a lot we can do in our own lives, and I’ll get to that in a bit. And there is a lot we can do across the food supply chain. At ReFED, the organization where I work, that is entirely dedicated to reducing the amount of food we waste, we have identified over 80 solutions that can help. Many of them are about prevention, about making sure that extra food does not occur in the first place, which is really our priority because prevention gives you the most bang for your buck, both environmentally and financially. After that, we look at donating food. And, only when that’s been exhausted, at feeding it to animals, composting it, or other recycling methods.",
      "G. There are so many successful examples out there of these solutions. One is a Nigerian company called ColdHubs. They build solar-powered cold rooms in markets and farms. And in places that don’t have electricity this extends shelf life dramatically, giving farmers more time to sell their product and therefore the ability to fetch a better price. They have saved thousands of tons of food so far, all while increasing farmer incomes by 60 percent.",
      "H. Another is Too Good To Go. It’s an app that restaurants and grocery stores can use to discount products at the last minute, before they might otherwise throw it out. Businesses, they get extra revenue, customers score a deal, and it has spread like wildfire. Now in 17 countries, they saved over 100 million meals last year alone.",
      "I. From a different angle, there’s Compass Group. It’s the largest food service company in the world, and they are busy trying a lot of unsexy things like tracking their waste, experimenting with smaller containers on buffets or offering different sized portions so that there’s a smaller option if you, say, don’t want a massive burrito. They’ve had a lot of success across the world, even decreasing waste up to 50 percent in some of their largest sites.",
      "J. These are just a few of the many solutions that are being tried and tested. Some are high-tech, some are low, many are win-win—and they’re starting to work. But it’s not enough, and it’s not fast enough. Countries and companies from around the world have signed on to the UN Sustainable Development Goal to cut food loss and waste in half by 2030. The impact of this goal would be enormous. It could avoid converting an area of land the size of Argentina into agriculture, saving one-third of the biodiversity that we’re projected to lose and avoiding as many emissions as taking every single car in the US and Canada off the road would.",
      "K. Yet, despite that incredible potential and some beacons of progress, overall, we are barely moving the needle. And unlike the energy and transportation sectors, attention and investment into food waste have been completely incommensurate with the opportunity it presents. There are so many solutions that are working out there. Let’s inject the funds to really scale them. ColdHubs has been quite successful across Nigeria, but we need that solution in every market without electricity in every country. Waste tracking, last-minute sale apps and other technologies are demonstrating they work. Let’s provide incentives so that every food business has those at their fingertips. And we need investment to spark new innovation as well.",
      "L. At ReFED, we estimate that for the US alone, it could take 18 billion dollars to fully scale solutions. A large investment. But when you are throwing hundreds of billions of food away, it actually can have a four-to-one return on that investment. And other incredible benefits, like providing four billion additional meals in food donations, creating 60,000 jobs, and huge water and greenhouse gas savings. And because so much of the action needs to happen across the food supply chain, we need food businesses to engage and prioritize this work. They can be doubling down on streamlining their operations and adopting new solutions as well.",
      "M. We also need policy. Ecuador, Japan, France and 10 states across the US are just some of the places that have laws that restrict food from going to landfills. Instead, they provide infrastructure for composting or other recycling methods, and that compost then goes on to improve soils and even sequester carbon, potentially. In addition, in some of those places, they’re seeing an increase in food donations and even closer tracking of waste. These laws should be everywhere.",
      "N. And then there’s us. One of my favorite bumper stickers says: “Hate traffic? You are traffic.” Well, we as consumers, are actually the largest source of food going to waste. More than grocery stores, farms or restaurants. And in the US, as a culture, we have also become really numb to it. You know, I could walk down the street and throw half a sandwich on a sidewalk and people would think I was crazy. But if I throw that same half sandwich in a garbage can, they wouldn’t think much of it. We need to be less accepting as a culture of wasting food, and we need to take steps in our own lives as well.",
      "O. But before I get to that, let me tell you what not to do. You are going to go out into the world now, and you are going to see food being wasted everywhere. And you’re going to want to, well, eat it. This is not what we’re going for, people. Trust me, I have tried. And I don’t think it’s what you’re going for either. Instead, here are five tips that you can try to manage your food better in your own lives.",
      "P. First, shopping. Shopping is really where we commit to food, and so we need to be careful not to overbuy. Old-school things like shopping lists and meal planning really help. And let me be clear, frozen pizza and takeout are totally legit as part of your plan.",
      "Q. Next, as I tell my friends at the end of dinner, love your leftovers. They are the only true free lunch. And when you get sick of them, you can move on to number three, which is freeze your food. Your freezer is like a magic pause button, and so many things can be frozen that you don’t think of: bread, milk, cheese and that half jar of pasta sauce you didn’t use.",
      "R. Next, use it up. In my house, this looks like my husband eating that peanut butter and jelly sandwich for dinner. But for you, it might be whipping up a stir fry with whatever veggies are wilting in your fridge. Whatever it is, be sure to shop your fridge before you restock it.",
      "S. And lastly, learn your labels. “Best by” and “enjoy by” are really just guesstimates of when food is at its best, they’re not an indication that it’s gone bad. So be sure to use your senses before you toss things.",
      "T. These strategies are not Earth-shattering. They’re things that many of our parents and grandparents did. And you can be sure that my son is learning them as well. Because as we tackle this massive climate crisis, reducing food waste really is the low-hanging fruit. But no matter how sustainably we grow that fruit, it’s only a good use of resources and nutrition if we all do our part to make sure that it actually gets eaten. Thank you."
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "castro",
    "short": "Reading · Castro et al.",
    "title": "Preventing household food waste through mobile applications",
    "cite": "Castro et al. (2023)",
    "kind": "Core reading",
    "reference": "Adapted from: Castro C., Chitikova, E., Magnani, G., Merkle, J., & Heitmayer, M. (2023). Less is more: Preventing household food waste through an integrated mobile application. Sustainability, 15(13), Article 10597. https://doi.org/10.3390/su151310597",
    "citeAs": "Cite as Castro et al. (2023) or (Castro et al., 2023).",
    "paragraphs": [
      "A. Across the global food-supply chain, approximately 1.3 billion tons of food are lost or wasted each year. This is equivalent to one-third of all edible food being disposed of across all stages from production to consumption (Gustavsson et al., 2021). Information and Communications Technologies (ICTs), such as mobile applications, have great potential to reduce household food waste given that they can be easily made available to a large proportion of consumers (Principato et al, 2021). These applications can help consumers to reduce food waste across three stages: acquisition, consumption and disposal.",
      "## Acquisition",
      "B. Despite the recent increase in online food shopping, most food is still purchased in physical stores, especially supermarkets (Saphores & Xu, 2021). According to Farr-Wharton et al. (2018), supermarkets are the main places where food waste happens because they encourage buying too much. Often, people buy things on impulse—buying things they didn’t plan to buy when entering the store. This accounts for nearly 60% of purchases (Mattila & Wirtz 2018). Buying on impulse is seen by shoppers as an unnecessary use of money and effort, often encouraged by store layouts.",
      "C. To effectively reduce food waste that results from buying too much at the store, it’s important to address the common cognitive biases and memory issues that affect shoppers. Apps like No Waste and Plus Fridge Pal are helpful as they allow consumers to track what they need and what they don’t, reducing unnecessary purchases. These apps also provide a summary of past shopping behaviours, showing which items were bought and not used, which helps lessen biases such as the present bias and planning fallacy.",
      "D. However, a challenge with this method is the need to enter product details and expiration dates into the app, which can be quite burdensome. Integrating a barcode scanner or connecting the app with online grocery delivery services could simplify this process. Additionally, using portion-ready food delivery services like Hello Fresh, or apps that sell food close to expiration like Too Good to Go, MyFoody, and FoodCloud, can help manage the amount of food bought. These services deliver the exact quantities needed for specific recipes, which not only offers convenience but also reduces the likelihood of over-purchasing in supermarkets.",
      "## Consumption",
      "E. During the consumption stage, people decide what food to eat, which ingredients to use, and how much to cook and serve (Block et al., 2018). Secondi et al. (2015) point out that a lot of food waste could be avoided if food was better portioned, managed, stored, and prepared. It’s also crucial to consider if people reuse leftovers, as this can greatly reduce food waste. The kitchen is the main area for these activities, but setups can vary, like a family home usually having a shared dining table, whereas student housing might not.",
      "F. In the consumption stage, the way people handle food waste is influenced by their fear of foodborne illnesses, not knowing how to check if food is still good to eat, confusing food labels, forgetting what they have in their kitchen, and social habits. To help reduce food waste, digital tools can be developed that make it easier to keep track of what’s in the kitchen, judge the quality of food, and manage storage better.",
      "G. Apps like NoWaste and Plus Fridge Pal give a clear view of what food you have and when it might go bad, which helps prevent food from being forgotten and thrown out. These apps can also guide you on how to understand food labels and check if food is still fresh. However, their effectiveness depends on how accurately users update their food inventory in the app. It’s also a challenge to ensure these apps don’t mistakenly label expired food as safe to eat.",
      "H. Another app, Plant Jammer, offers recipes that help use up food that’s about to expire. This not only helps reduce waste but also suggests the right amount of food to prepare. Apps can also raise awareness about the effects of food waste and promote habits that are good for the environment. They can show the nutritional benefits of leftovers and offer simple ways to turn them into new meals, which can make leftovers more appealing and reduce the risk people see in eating them.",
      "## Disposal",
      "I. Disposal happens when people decide not to keep certain foods. Typically, they can throw it away, recycle it (by composting), or give it to someone else. Composting helps reduce the environmental impact of food waste but doesn’t directly cut down the amount of waste. This is why it’s not the main focus of our strategies. Research across EU countries shows that people who sort their waste tend to report less food waste (Secondi et al., 2015). However, in the US, some people feel that composting justifies wasting food, as 41% reported not being concerned about wasting food because they compost (Neff at al. 2015).",
      "J. While composting’s impact on food waste reduction is not clear-cut, sharing food offers a significant opportunity to decrease waste. Morton and colleagues (2008) discovered that people living in rural low-income areas in the US often share food with their family, friends, and neighbours. In contrast, those in urban low-income areas usually get food through community sharing programs. Although Morton’s study doesn’t go into detail about the differences between rural and urban areas, it suggests that rural communities are designed to encourage more personal interactions, which might lead to more food sharing. Urban areas, however, tend to use digital solutions.",
      "K. According to Choi et al. (2019), platforms that facilitate the sharing of leftover and near-expiration food can benefit consumers, retailers, and suppliers by reducing food waste. There are apps like EquoEvento, FoodSharing.de and IFoodShare, that help with this. However, these apps are mostly used for business reasons and don’t fully apply to households. When expanding food sharing platforms to households, building trust and interpersonal connection is crucial. Technology that fosters a sense of community is one way to build trust among users. Another way is to let users rate and review each other.",
      "L. Overall, digital tools such as mobile applications can help consumers reduce food waste. They can do this by supporting people to make better decisions when people buy, prepare and dispose of food. Although the apps currently available in the space can be useful, they also have some limitations in their functionality and ease of access."
    ],
    "figure": "assets/week4/app-features.svg",
    "figureAlt": "Four app functions: smart inventory, smart recipes, food-sharing hub and portion-ready food delivery, with what each can do.",
    "figureCaption": "App functionalities discussed in the article.",
    "figureCredit": "Adapted from Castro et al. (2023), Sustainability, 15(13), Article 10597."
  },
  {
    "id": "huang",
    "short": "Mediation · Huang et al.",
    "title": "Analysing consumer motivations and behaviors towards upcycled food",
    "cite": "Huang et al. (2024)",
    "kind": "Mediation reading",
    "reference": "Adapted from: Huang, W. S., Tsai, T. Y., Lai, K. L., & Chen, H. S. (2024). Analyzing consumer motivations and behaviors towards upcycled food from an environmental sustainability perspective. Agriculture, 14(11), Article 1967. https://doi.org/10.3390/agriculture14111967",
    "citeAs": "Cite as Huang et al. (2024) or (Huang et al., 2024).",
    "paragraphs": [
      "A. Food production and related waste have a significant impact on climate change and global food security (Garske et al., 2020; Rodriguez Garcia & Raghavan 2022) According to data from the Food and Agriculture Organization (2023), approximately one-third of the food produced globally is wasted during the production and consumption processes, accounting for approximately 8–10% of total greenhouse gas emissions, and up to 800 million people worldwide suffer from hunger due to insufficient food. To address these problems and meet the Sustainable Development Goals of ending hunger and promoting sustainable practices, using upcycled food is crucial.",
      "B. Upcycled food uses non-conventional food materials in a way that is good for the environment and can be traced back to its source (Upcycled Food Association 2023). For example, grains left over from making beer can be turned into flour. This flour can then be used to make cakes, cookies, and other products. Consumers are realizing that using upcycled ingredients can reduce food waste and help the environment. Studies have shown that upcycled food products keep their nutritional value and help reduce waste, supporting both health and environmental sustainability (Thorsen et al., 2022; Grasso et al. 2021).",
      "C. In Taiwan, the effect of upcycled food on how consumers act is not well-known. This study aimed to understand this by using the Value-Attitude-Behaviour (VAB) model. The VAB model looks at factors that affect consumers’ intention to purchase environmentally friendly products, such as a how healthy a product is, the products perceived green value, product novelty, and ethical consumption. The model was used in this study to measure how Taiwanese consumers view and might act towards upcycled food. The findings of the study show that health considerations greatly shape consumer attitudes towards upcycled food. These considerations then encourage environmentally friendly actions. This suggests that consumers who value health might be interested in buying upcycled products. Therefore, food businesses should clearly advertise the health benefits of upcycled food, such as their higher nutritional value or lack of heavily processed ingredients. Working with nutritionists and health organizations could also build trust and credibility, perhaps by promoting health-focused recipes or eating plans approved by health professionals.",
      "D. The study also highlights the importance of ethical consumption in encouraging green actions. It suggests that businesses should get and show certifications for ethical production on their product packaging and promotions. Ethical production may include things like clear supply chain management, animal welfare, environmental protection and trade practices. For example, Tony’s Chocolonely, a Dutch chocolate company, shows its commitment to ethical standards through fair trade and B-Corp certifications on its packaging. The company also promotes transparency in its cocoa supply chain on its packaging and website, showing detailed interactions with specific farms and their production processes.",
      "E. Interestingly, the study found that product novelty and the perceived green value have little effect on green actions among Taiwanese consumers. This result was not expected as previous studies have found a strong relationship between green value and green behaviours (Zhuang, et al., 2021). However, Imtiyaz et al. (2021) point out that consumers may value practical use and cost-effectiveness over a product’s green value. This finding suggests a shift in marketing strategy that emphasizes the practical benefits and relevance to daily life of upcycled food products should be considered. This may help to increase consumer acceptance and market penetration of upcycled foods.",
      "F. This study adds to our understanding of consumer attitudes to upcycled food. Emphasising the practical aspects of upcycled food, such as the health and cost benefits for consumers may help to increase the uptake of such foods. Although consumers do not appear to be motivated by the green value of upcycled food, increased uptake of these products will nonetheless have benefits for the environment. Reducing food waste has the potential to reduce the greenhouse gas emissions associated with food disposal in addition to increasing the amount of food available for consumption, offering a win-win for both the environment and consumers."
    ],
    "figure": "assets/week4/vab-model.svg",
    "figureAlt": "Figure 1, the Value–Attitude–Behaviour model: health values lead to attitude towards upcycled foods, which leads to green behavioural intentions; green perceived value, product novelty and moral consumption also point to green behavioural intentions.",
    "figureCaption": "Figure 1. VAB model.",
    "figureCredit": "Redrawn from Huang et al. (2024), Agriculture, 14(11), Article 1967."
  },
  {
    "id": "negotiation-ending2",
    "short": "Discussion sample · ending 2",
    "title": "Research Summary Discussion — alternative ending (students don’t all agree)",
    "cite": "DEC15 sample recording",
    "kind": "Discussion transcript",
    "reference": "Sample students’ discussion recorded for DEC15 (Step 5, Part 2, alternative ending). Question: Which of the consequences of the modern agricultural production system is the most serious? a) health problems b) environmental damage c) animal rights violations.",
    "note": "Roles: Student 1 (UK focus), Student 4 (Kenya focus), Student 5 (Netherlands focus). Listen first; then read to check.",
    "paragraphs": [
      "Student 1 (UK Focus): It's clear that all these consequences are serious, but it seems that none of these issues can be viewed in isolation. Each factor affects the other.",
      "Student 4 (Kenya Focus): Yeah, but the question does ask which of the consequences is the most serious. Which one do you think we should choose?",
      "Student 1 (UK Focus): I really think environmental damage is the most pressing issue. The way agriculture contributes to soil degradation, deforestation, and pollution is such a big concern.",
      "Student 4 (Kenya Focus): I don’t really agree with you there. I’m not sure that we should be so concerned about soil. Don’t you think that as human beings, the direct impact on human health should be our biggest worry? The effect of chemicals and antibiotic-resistant bacteria are massive threats to our health. Last year I got really sick, and I’m sure it was from the chemicals used in the food I was eating.",
      "Student 1 (UK Focus): Yes, I admit that those are big problems, but I think you’re being a bit dismissive. Soil degradation leads to reduced fertility and affects the land’s ability to support agricultural crops. According to the United Nations Environment Programme, up to 33% of the world's soils are already degraded, and over 90% could become degraded by 2050. Besides, I’m not only talking about soil degradation. We can’t deny the negative impacts of polluting the water we need to drink and cutting down the trees that create the oxygen we need to breathe.",
      "Student 4 (Kenya Focus): I guess you’re right. Those issues can’t be ignored, and they’re actually directly linked to human health.",
      "Student 5 (Netherlands Focus): Both of you have valid points, but we're completely ignoring the ethical implications here. The industrial farming of animals is horrendous. These animals suffer extreme cruelty—from confinement to the way they are treated and eventually killed.",
      "Student 4 (Kenya Focus): That’s not always the case though. My family owns a dairy farm, and they treat the cows very well. If they had to give the animals more space, then it would be really bad for the business.",
      "Student 5 (Netherlands Focus): But it sounds like that is a small-scale operation which does not accurately represent this global issue. In our research we learnt that in the Netherlands about 37 land-based animals are killed per person annually. That doesn’t even include seafood! Surely that must be the most serious consequence of agricultural production.",
      "Student 1 (UK Focus): Hmmmm… I’m not sure either of you is considering the broader picture. Environmental damage affects not just us but every living organism. If we continue like this, we'll have no viable land for farming, nor clean water. It impacts biodiversity, climate change—everything!",
      "Student 4 (Kenya Focus): That’s true, environmental issues are significant, but health problems are immediate. Look at the health care costs and the impact on life expectancy. If we don’t prioritize human health, we can’t maintain a functioning society.",
      "Student 5 (Netherlands Focus): I understand that human-centric problems are important because they affect us, but violating animal rights is an ethical issue. I mean, meat is murder! Shouldn't ethics guide all our other decisions?",
      "Student 1 (UK Focus): You’re right. Ethics are important, but without a healthy environment, there won’t be any animals to protect, or humans to keep healthy! It’s all interconnected, but the root starts with our environment.",
      "Student 5 (Netherlands Focus): Yeah, I suppose you have a point there. Protecting the environment does also mean protecting animals. Maybe this is the most serious consequence.",
      "Student 4 (Kenya Focus): I’m sorry but I just don’t agree with that. I still argue that it starts with our health. If people are sick, how can they fight for animals or the environment? Public health drives the ability of societies to function and push for change in other areas.",
      "Student 1 (UK Focus): Look, it’s been almost 15 minutes. We’ll be out of time soon.",
      "Student 4 (Kenya Focus): Do you think it’s okay if we don’t reach an agreement?",
      "Student 5 (Netherlands Focus): I think so. The teacher said the most important thing is to engage in the discussion."
    ]
  }
]);
